  "narrative": {
    "intro": "General Dynamics Information Technology has $3.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The firm won 881 awards so far, with an average award size of $4.1M, and obligations fell 21.6% year over year. Obligations in FY2026 to date sit below the FY24 peak of $4.8B and below FY25 totals of $4.6B, following a multiyear rise from $2.4B in FY21 through $4.3B in FY23. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in FY2026 to date at $1.2B, followed by the General Services Administration at $788M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $753M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest market at $1.7B, with Custom Computer Programming Services at $646M and Engineering Services at $486M. The distribution across agencies and NAICS shows a heavy share in IT and engineering work, while other agencies and services contribute smaller but material amounts. These facts frame current-year performance and the principal buyers and markets for the contractor.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are General Dynamics Information Technology's FY2026 obligations across agencies?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for $1.2B of FY2026 to date obligations. The General Services Administration and the Department of Health and Human Services together add $788M and $753M respectively, showing that three agencies make up a large portion of obligations. Smaller but notable buyers include the Department of Homeland Security at $175M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $162M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and contract types drive General Dynamics Information Technology's revenue in FY2026?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services is the largest market at $1.7B, followed by Custom Computer Programming Services at $646M and Engineering Services at $486M. Computing Infrastructure Providers, Data Processing, Web Hosting, and Related Services account for $160M, while consulting and support services combine for smaller shares in the tens of millions. This mix shows a strong emphasis on IT services and engineering work."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does General Dynamics Information Technology make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "General Dynamics Information Technology has $3.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The company won 881 awards so far in FY2026 to date, with an average award size of $4.1M, and obligations fell 21.6% year over year. Historical obligations were $4.6B in FY25 and $4.8B in FY24, down from those prior-year peaks."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from General Dynamics Information Technology?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense, General Services Administration, and Department of Health and Human Services buy the most from General Dynamics Information Technology in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense accounts for $1.2B, the General Services Administration accounts for $788M, and the Department of Health and Human Services accounts for $753M. Other FY2026 to date buyers include the Department of Homeland Security at $175M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $162M."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does General Dynamics Information Technology sell to the government?",
      "a": "General Dynamics Information Technology sells largely IT and engineering services to the federal government. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest market at $1.7B, Custom Computer Programming Services is $646M, and Engineering Services is $486M. Additional offerings include computing infrastructure and various consulting and support services in the tens to low hundreds of millions."
